<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/tim-cook/">Tim Cook</a> is visiting China for the third time this year, with talks in Guangdong focused on expanding Apple's manufacturing and sales operations, the <a href="https://www.scmp.com/tech/tech-trends/article/3370500/apples-tim-cook-kicks-china-visit-amid-mixed-signals-new-iphone-demand"><em>South China Morning Post</em></a> reports.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/Tim-Cook-Chengdu-China.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1041453" /><br/>
<br/>
This is Cook's first China trip in his capacity as Apple's executive chairman. He <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/01/john-ternus-is-now-apple-ceo/">moved into the position</a> on September 1 when John Ternus took over as CEO. Apple has said part of Cook's work involves relationships with government policymakers.<br/>
<br/>
On October 11, Cook reportedly held talks with Guangdong governor Meng Fanli. Suppliers and partners located in Guangdong play a major role in Apple's business in China, and Cook is said to have expressed interest in doing more locally through smart manufacturing and additional sales channels. Meng said the province would support further expansion, including research and development.<br/>
<br/>
In Guangzhou, a creator used an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> to capture martial arts footage while Cook watched. Cook posted "Great to be back in China!" on <a href="https://www.weibo.com/timcook">Weibo</a> on Saturday. Customers also reported a visit to one of Guangzhou's Apple Stores on Sunday.<br/>
<br/>
Cook traveled to Chengdu and Beijing <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/18/apple-ceo-tim-cook-visits-china/">in March</a> as Apple celebrated its 50th anniversary, and addressed the China Development Forum. In May, he was among the business leaders who accompanied President Donald Trump for talks with Chinese President Xi Jinping.<br/>
<br/>
